Count every document at the length the index stores for it

Applies to vchord_bm25 0.3.0 (upstream tag 0.3.0, commit c610b7ab).

The metapage keeps doc_term_cnt, the sum of document lengths, and BM25 takes
the average document length from it. An insert added the document's exact
length (insert.rs) and a build summed exact lengths (builder.rs), but
ambulkdelete (vacuum.rs) subtracted field_norm_reader.read(i): the document's
one-byte length code, not a length. The code equals the length up to 40 and
compresses above it (136 to 143 is code 62, 300 is code 72), so a deleted
document left its length minus its code behind: 1 at 41, 14 at 64, 43 at 100,
77 at 139. The average grew with every update and delete that VACUUM cleaned
up, until the index was rebuilt.

The index stores only the code, so VACUUM cannot take back an exact length.
Subtracting the length the code stands for is not enough either: the code
rounds a length down to the start of its bucket, so a replaced document still
left up to a bucket's width behind, and replacing every row once raised the
average by 3 to 4% for lengths between 41 and 600.

This patch makes every side count the length the code stands for,
id_to_fieldnorm(fieldnorm_to_id(len)): the build, the insert and VACUUM. What
VACUUM subtracts is then exactly what was added, and the sum stays the sum of
the remaining documents' stored lengths however often they are replaced. Those
are also the lengths BM25 scores each document with, so the average is now
the mean of the lengths it is compared against, a little below the mean of
exact lengths. No on-disk format or SQL changes.

Measured: 1,000 rows inserted, deleted and vacuumed left doc_term_cnt at
915,973 against 2,973 true, an average length of 3,578 against 11.61.

